from its feverish inspiration to strange  early casting discussions to an iconic  shot that almost got the crew in trouble  with the law here are a few facts you  might not have known about the making of  the Terminator some films begin with the  story while others begin with an image  in the case of the Terminator it began  with a literal fever dream after an  awful experience filming piranhas -  writer director James Cameron retreated  to Rome to recover he told Entertainment  Weekly nightmares are a business asset  that's the way I look at it I was sick I  was broke I had a high fever and I had a  dream about this metal death figure  coming out of a fire and the implication  was that it had been stripped of its  skin by the fire and exposed for what it  really was when I have some particularly  vivid image I'll draw it or I'll write  some notes and that goes on to this day  Cameron sketched his fever vision and  eventually showed the images to young  producer Gale and heard the Terminator  was born with the idea set in their  minds Cameron and Hurd elaborated on the  film's script then began to shop at  around two studios as a low-budget  guerilla style film the Cameron would  direct and Hurd would produce to keep  costs low they decided to have Lance  Henriksen play The Terminator  but when Orion Pictures came on board  with financing they had a much crazier  idea Oh Jay Simpson Cameron said no to  that idea but reluctantly agreed to take  a meeting with Orion head Mike Medavoy  stop choice to play the heroic time  traveler Kyle Reese met a voice choice  Conan the Barbarian star Arnold  Schwarzenegger  Cameron was skeptical but came away  impressed by Schwarzenegger zai Diaz for  the character of the Terminator instead  of offering him the role of Reese  Cameron offered Schwarzenegger the role  of terminator Schwarzenegger initially  feared playing the villain would hurt  his leading man image but Cameron won  him over  Schwarzenegger told Entertainment Weekly  but Cameron said that he'd shoot it in  such a way that all the evil stuff that  I do will be totally excused by  audiences because I'm a cool machine and  so cool that some of the people will  cheer shorts and Egger took the role he  would prove to be the best decision of  his career he wasn't the only second  pick in the film though with  Schwarzenegger off the board numerous  stars were considered for the role of  Rhys including everyone from Mel Gibson  and Kurt Russell to Bruce Springsteen  Superman star Christopher Reeve declined  a million-dollar offer to play the role  but perhaps the strangest star to pass  on the film was the musicians sting  after being offered a cool $350,000 to  play the role sting ultimately passed  because of prior obligations including a  role in the epic science fiction flop  dune opening the door for Michael Biehn  who would team with Cameron again for  aliens in 1986 and the abyss in 1989  even some of the talent behind the  scenes were second choices Cameron and  Hurd originally wanted legendary makeup  artist Dick Smith to design the  terminators look he said no but pointed  them towards another rising star in the  business Stan Winston who would go on to  win four Academy Awards for his effects  work including two for Terminator 2  though she would go on to become an  action badass in subsequent films in the  first Terminator movie Sarah Connor  spends the whole movie running away and  that was a problem for star Linda  Hamilton because just before shooting  she broke her ankle tearing some  ligaments - just for good measure to  make things easier on Hamilton  urghhh shuffled the production schedule  around so that many of the scenes that  involve running were shot near the end  of principal photography but it was  still far from easy Hurd told  Entertainment Weekly she had to run  throughout the movie and it was mostly  Linda by the way  on a broken ankle if it had to be  wrapped every day the Terminator  franchise has endured for more than  three decades and through it all one  line has remained a signature not just  for the franchise but for Schwarzenegger  as a major action star I'll be back  ironically Schwarzenegger tried to  change the line I couldn't pronounce  really well this aisle so I just  considered that Jim can miniatures just  say I will be back  Cameron refused to budge this is none  Isis look I didn't tell you how to act  don't tell me how to write I wrote the  line I'll be back Cameron shot several  takes of the scene and had  Schwarzenegger deliver the same line a  different way each time then picked the  best take almost immediately after the  film was released fans were approaching  Schwarzenegger on the street to ask him  to repeat it Cameron and Hurd shot the  film guerilla style filming mini scenes  without location permits and that almost  got the production in hot water with the  law during the filming of the final  scene when a cop crashed the party Hurd  told Entertainment Weekly he said I need  to see your permit for filming here we  didn't have one we said oh officer were  making a UCLA student film we didn't  know you needed permits and he said okay  you're fine just take the camera off the  road I don't know if that cop ever  figured it out wherever he is we thanked  him for not shutting us down  in the summer of 1984 as the Terminator  was nearing completion Cameron took a  rough cut to show the bosses at Orion  they were less than impressed with  Cameron calling the screening quote  disastrous Byrd told Entertainment  Weekly they had such little faith in the  movie that they didn't want to screen it  for critics the head of marketing almost  said as much and if you were Orion and  you had Amadeus which they had released  five weeks before and which did go on to  win Best Picture  well I can imagine them saying Amadeus  The Terminator which one doesn't fit I  can't blame them now but at the time I  was devastated when they didn't like it  in the terminators closing credits  there's one credit that may seem odd to  viewers not familiar with how it came  about  it reads acknowledgment to the works of  Harlan Ellison Ellison of course was a  legendary science fiction author who is  almost as legendary for being hint  Kuras so when he discovered the  similarities between the Terminator and  to Ellison stories for the 60s TV series  Outer Limits soldier and demon with a  glass hand he contacted his lawyers  we've been altered to look like humans  it was necessary to come back into time  and find you what do you want with me  you're the last hope of earth Cameron  publicly denied ripping off Ellison  stories but Ellison reached a settlement  out of court with the studio that  included both money and that credit  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

